    {
     "from": "COURT_BLUE",
     "to": "ADJUDICATE",
     "utc": "2026-08-17T19:53:20Z",
     "artifact": "/Users/ajay/exalted/signalos/desk/data/court_artifacts/TCPC_COURT_BLUE_202608171953.md",
     "note": null
    },
    {
     "from": "ADJUDICATE",
     "to": "KILLED",
     "utc": "2026-08-17T19:54:43Z",
     "artifact": "desk/data/court_artifacts/TCPC_COURT_BLUE_202608171953.md",
     "note": "ADJUDICATED mark-integrity: FLAT on size/liquidity; anchor unproven both ways; sources-and-uses unreconciled"
    }
   ],
   "context": "BLUE BENCH — TCPC. TWO JOBS, and the second is not in red's record.\n\nJOB 1 — PROSECUTE RED'S ANCHOR, WHICH IS THE STRONGEST EVIDENCE IN THIS FILE. Red found that Pantheon paid 88.8% of TCPC's OWN 6/30/26 carrying value ($57.1M written off $511.9M), and that the flattering '95% of gross fair value' is measured against a STALE 12/31/25 mark. That is an EXTERNAL, ARM'S-LENGTH PRICE on this manager's own book — under the desk's mispricing-artifact doctrine an external same-event anchor outranks ANY inference from PIK ratios or fee structures, so red's route to the answer is better than the brief's. Red then applies it to the retained 56-name book: pro-forma NAV $5.90 -> ~$4.86, collapsing the discount at $4.05 from ~38% to ~17%.\nTEST EXACTLY TWO THINGS, because red named both as its own change-my-mind and neither is resolved:\n  (a) PRICE OR FEES? Red's own condition: if more than $25M of the $57.1M was transaction EXPENSES rather than price, the implied mark moves to ~94% and Finding 2 materially softens. Pull the Transaction Agreement / 8-K/A and separate expenses from consideration. This single number decides whether the anchor is an 11% mark haircut or a ~6% one.\n  (b) ADVERSE SELECTION or BOOK-WIDE? A portfolio bid on a SOLD sleeve is not the same as a mark on the RETAINED book — buyers demand liquidity discounts on bulk transfers, and sellers choose what to sell. Test whether the sold sleeve was representative: compare its composition (non-accrual share, PIK share, seniority mix, industry) against the retained 56 names. If the sold book was adversely selected or bulk-discounted, red's read-through to retained NAV is overstated.\n\nJOB 2 — THE MARK-INTEGRITY LEGS RED NEVER TOUCHED (zero mentions of non-accruals, the incentive fee, 36th Street, or Kellermeyer). These come from a 12-manager primary-filing study run this session and they cut the OTHER way; adjudicate them against red's anchor rather than ignoring them:\n  (1) TCPC is the cohort's HARSHEST recogniser — implied mark on its own non-accruals 19.4 cents (7.4% of cost at 1.6% of fair value) vs PNNT 31.1c, FSK 47.9c, WHF 50.4c, MFIC 55.1c.\n  (2) THE FEE MECHANISM RUNS IN REVERSE: cumulative total-return hurdle unmet since 2013 = ZERO incentive fee. Marking up advances a fee it cannot collect. The same test that condemns NMFC (whose fee counts PIK and ignores unrealized depreciation) exonerates TCPC.\n  (3) PIK fell 48% in dollars, 16.7% of NII and FALLING, vs NMFC 41.8% and FSK 34.9% both RISING. It CUT the dividend to stay covered rather than fund from spillover.\n  (4) On the cohort's most contested credit, Kellermeyer Bergensons, TCPC carries its stub at 7.2 cents while FSK carries the same borrower's senior tranche at 98.4 cents ACCRUING.\nIF RED'S ANCHOR AND THESE LEGS BOTH HOLD, SAY SO AND RECONCILE THEM — 'conservative marks that are STILL 11% above the clearing price' is a coherent and important finding, not a contradiction.\n\nTHE THREE KILLERS EITHER WAY: (a) asset coverage 160.3% vs the 150% floor = only ~6.75% markdown before breach, the tightest cushion in the cohort — recompute it from the balance sheet; (b) 36th Street Capital is CONTROLLED at 20.8% OF NET ASSETS carried at 144.9% of cost with no third-party price (mitigants: legacy mark, ~$55K of 2026 appreciation, adviser earns nothing on it); (c) NAV 9.18->8.71->8.71->7.07->6.72->6.58 over five quarters = -28%, so the markdown may not be finished — VALUE-LADDER FLOW GATE APPLIES, do not bid into a markdown still in progress.\nTAX (mandatory, the ARE court skipped its): BDC distributions are ORDINARY income; state the after-tax carry at ~50% marginal against the desk's 3.5% bar.\nTAPE: discount was 48.9% at quarter-end ($3.36 vs $6.58) and is 38.4% now ($4.05) — ~10 points of re-rating already happened. Evidence base: desk/data/bdc_marks/*.json (12 managers, DIRECT EDGAR, every schedule re-summed and tied to the balance sheet)."
  },
