   "context": "STRUCTURAL THESIS COURT — LIND. READ THIS FIRST: this name did NOT arrive from a dislocation screen. It arrived from a THESIS. A prior TRAP_VERIFY killed it as 'SCREEN-MISCLASSIFIED' by auditing a screen row that does not exist; that verdict is VACATED. DO NOT kill on 'not cheap', 'screened at the windfall', or 'the drawdown is stale' — none of those address the claim.\n\nTHE CLAIM TO ADJUDICATE, and it is price-agnostic: eco-tourism's best assets are supply-capped BY REGULATORS, and a licence to operate inside a cap is a quasi-monopoly the market prices as cyclical travel. Lindblad holds Galapagos vessel licences (Ecuador's cupo regime — a fixed, grandfathered number), Antarctic capacity constrained by IAATO rules and ice-class tonnage scarcity, and a National Geographic brand licence. THE COURT'S JOB IS TO VALUE THAT, AND THEN SAY AT WHAT PRICE IT IS OWNABLE. 'Not ownable at $32.77' is an acceptable conclusion ONLY if it comes with the price at which it would be — derived from the moat, not from a multiple screen.\n\nTHE DECISIVE QUESTION: WHAT FRACTION OF ENTERPRISE VALUE IS THE LICENCE VERSUS THE HULL? Read the primary — Ecuador's Galapagos National Park cupo/licence regime, the 10-K concession-renewal risk factors, and the Nat Geo agreement's term and exclusivity. Grandfathered and NON-TRANSFERABLE means replacement cost is effectively infinite and this is not a cruise line. Renewable, transferable or politically revocable means it is a boat business with a good brand and a renewal clock. FIND THE DATES.\n\nESTABLISHED FACTS — carried from the vacated trap-verify, all primary-sourced, DO NOT RE-DERIVE THEM (they are good facts about a different question, and they constrain the answer):\n  * 65,515,419 shares at ~$31.74 = ~$2.079B; debt $675.0M less cash $318.9M -> EV ~$2.435B against FY26 GUIDED adj EBITDA $130-140M = 17.4-18.7x.\n  * Q2 was a RECORD: net yield $1,294, 91% occupancy (best in a decade), capacity +12%, adj EBITDA +31%. Tape +179% off the 52-week low. So the moat, if real, is being valued at a CYCLE PEAK — your valuation must normalise, not extrapolate.\n  * Stockholders' DEFICIT $(195.8)M. Unearned passenger revenue $439.9M EXCEEDS total cash $318.9M ($46.0M restricted). Net debt $356.1M.\n  * H1 OCF $108.5M of which the unearned-revenue build was +$78.4M = 72%; capex only $14.9M (no newbuild in period). GAAP: H1 net income to common $4.6M ($0.07); Q2 a $(1.4)M LOSS.\n  * DESK CONNECTOR expedition_inventory (built for this court, live 2026-08-17, Lindblad's own Algolia index): 72 itineraries / 1,986 departures; 59.6% sold out departure-weighted; ZERO fare cuts anywhere (price==discounted 72/72); the ONLY promotion in the book is '50% Reduced Deposit' on 66/72 — a TERMS concession that HALVES the deposit float generating 72% of OCF. Regional dispersion: Arctic 0% sold out on 82 departures (100% promoted), Baja 0%, Antarctica 31%, vs Galapagos 70.9% on 1,145 departures, South America 70.3% on 1,271, Asia 94.4%.\n\nWHAT THE VERDICT MUST CONTAIN:\n(1) A RULING ON THE MOAT: real and durable, or a renewal clock? Cite the licence regime from primary. If the cupo is grandfathered and non-transferable, say what that is worth — a licence with no replacement cost inside a hard cap is not valued at an EBITDA multiple.\n(2) A NORMALISED VALUATION, not a peak one. 91% occupancy is the best in a decade; what does the moat earn mid-cycle?\n(3) THE PRICE AT WHICH IT IS OWNABLE, derived from (1) and (2). This is the deliverable.\n(4) The deposit-float mechanic priced: halving deposits on 92% of the book attacks the cash engine — quantify the working-capital hit and whether the moat survives it.\n(5) The Arctic 0%-sold-out anomaly: booking-curve position, itinerary-count inflation, or genuine demand failure in high-cost polar product?\n(6) DIVERGENCE, NOT COVERAGE (§DIVERGENCE-NOT-COVERAGE): extract the street's load-bearing number for the forward season and put ours beside it with a checkable mechanism. Never kill on analyst count.\nFETCH DISCIPLINE: desk/sec_fetch.py DIRECT; r.jina.ai truncates 10-Qs before the notes. UNVERIFIED beats a fetch failure dressed as a finding."
  },
