   "screen_row": {
    "ticker": "MTN",
    "name": "Vail Resorts",
    "px": 146.6,
    "mcap": 5220000000.0,
    "dd52": -0.114,
    "sector": "Ski resorts / pre-sold pass"
   },
   "history": [
    {
     "from": "COURT_RED",
     "to": "COURT_BLUE",
     "utc": "2026-08-18T01:23:49Z",
     "artifact": "/Users/ajay/exalted/signalos/desk/data/court_artifacts/MTN_COURT_RED_202608180123.md",
     "note": null
    },
    {
     "from": "COURT_BLUE",
     "to": "ADJUDICATE",
     "utc": "2026-08-18T01:31:34Z",
     "artifact": "/Users/ajay/exalted/signalos/desk/data/court_artifacts/MTN_COURT_BLUE_202608180131.md",
     "note": null
    },
    {
     "from": "ADJUDICATE",
     "to": "KILLED",
     "utc": "2026-08-18T01:34:29Z",
     "artifact": null,
     "note": "ADJUDICATED eco-tourism thesis court: AVOID"
    }
   ],
   "context": "PRE-SOLD SEASON THESIS — MTN (Vail Resorts). Tape $146.60, ~$5.22B, 11.4% off its 52-week high. THIS IS A THESIS ROW, NOT A DISLOCATION SCREEN — do not kill it for being 'not cheap' or for a stale drawdown; those critique a screen row that does not exist.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE: Vail pre-sells season passes in SPRING for the FOLLOWING winter, so revenue is deferred float — the same shape as an expedition cruise's deposits — and the model deliberately DECOUPLES the sold year's pass revenue from that winter's weather. That is the strategy and it works. The exposure is NOT the sold year. It is the NEXT pass cycle and in-resort ancillary spend, both of which run on the snow customers actually experienced.\n\nDESK-BUILT EVIDENCE, generated for this court (connector resort_snowpack, USDA NRCS SNOTEL, free daily, stations sited ON the resorts; output at verticals/buyside_dd/outputs/resort_snowpack/):\n  THE SEASON CUSTOMERS JUST EXPERIENCED RAN AT ~55% OF MEDIAN PEAK SNOW-WATER-EQUIVALENT.\n    Vail Mountain 9.5\" vs 19.7\" median = 48%   |   Fremont Pass (Summit) 7.9\" vs 17.2\" = 46%\n    Copper Mountain (Keystone/Breck proxy) 12.6\" vs 23.5\" = 54%   |   Tahoe 14.0\" vs 19.2\" = 73%\n    (11 water years of history per station; Park City station unreachable — a NAMED gap, not a zero.)\n  THE MECHANISM TO ADJUDICATE: a ~55%-of-median winter is the memory into which the CURRENT pass cycle is being sold. Test it against what the company actually reports — pass units AND dollars are disclosed ahead of the season, which is the rare case where a physical leading indicator has a published counterpart. Does the pass-sales disclosure show unit softness, price softness, or neither?\n  HONEST LIMITS the court must honour: a SNOTEL station is a POINT, not the skiable terrain (good on season shape, weak on absolute conditions); and snowpack conditions DEMAND, not the contractually insulated sold-year pass revenue.\n\nWHAT THE VERDICT MUST CONTAIN:\n(1) THE PASS-SALES READ: units vs dollars vs prior year, from the company's own pre-season disclosure. If units are holding while dollars soften, that is a PRICE concession; if Vail is instead easing TERMS (payment plans, credits, insurance, deferred billing) that is the LIND deposit-concession mechanic — invisible in price, and it moves risk onto the operator. Classify which.\n(2) ANCILLARY vs PASS: lift ticket, ski school, dining, lodging all move with actual visitation and are NOT insulated. Quantify the ancillary exposure to a 55%-of-median season.\n(3) EPIC PASS AS A MOAT — and apply the LIND test: is it a genuine switching-cost network, or is it rentable/replicable (Ikon)? Did Vail BUY its scarcity (resort acquisitions at disclosed prices), and does that price cap it?\n(4) THE DURATION QUESTION: snow reliability is a long-term terminal-value issue, not a one-season one. Is any part of the portfolio structurally marginal on elevation/latitude?\n(5) BALANCE SHEET + the deferred-revenue float: leverage, and what a weak pass cycle does to a business that collects cash before it delivers.\n(6) DIVERGENCE: the street's number for next-season pass revenue and EBITDA, and ours, with the snowpack as the mechanism.\n\nLESSONS FROM THE LIND COURT, 2026-08-18 — these are BINDING, and one is a correction of the adjudicator:\n* REFUTING A MOAT IS NOT A BEAR CASE. Capacity expansion falsifies a supply-cap premise but says nothing bad about a business — operators add capacity because they expect demand. THE TEST THAT SEPARATES THEM IS THE PRICE SERIES: absorbed expansion at intact pricing is a GROWING MARKET; expansion PLUS discounting is the bear case. Do not write up supply growth as negative without checking price first.\n* PRICE vs TERMS CONCESSIONS ARE DIFFERENT ANIMALS. An operator can concede on TERMS (deposit easing, payment plans, free cancellation) while leaving headline price untouched — invisible in any price series, but it shifts working-capital float and cancellation risk onto the operator. Lindblad had ZERO fare cuts across 72 itineraries and a universal '50% Reduced Deposit'. Classify every promotion as FARE or TERMS.\n* A MOAT THE ISSUER BOUGHT IS PRICED. If the allegedly scarce asset was acquired in a disclosed deal, that price caps it (LIND paid $17M for the Galapagos permit vehicle -> franchise ~2-7% of EV). Sibling tells: renting the 'scarce' capacity from a competitor, and allocating capital AWAY from it.\n* DIVERGENCE, NOT COVERAGE (§DIVERGENCE-NOT-COVERAGE): never kill on analyst count. Extract the STREET'S LOAD-BEARING NUMBER for the operative period AND its range, state ours beside it with a CHECKABLE MECHANISM. The kill is 'we agree with the street'. LIND died on exactly this.\n* SIZE IS NOT A DISQUALIFIER: compute days-to-build as position/(1% of ADV) against a ~$1.03M book before any unownability claim.\n* FETCH DISCIPLINE: desk/sec_fetch.py DIRECT. r.jina.ai truncates 10-Qs BEFORE the notes and produced two false absence findings in one day. UNVERIFIED beats a fetch failure dressed as a finding.\n* If a desk connector returns nothing or errors, say DEGRADED and name it — do not record an infrastructure failure as an analytic absence."
  },
