     "utc": "2026-08-18T01:30:58Z",
     "artifact": "/Users/ajay/exalted/signalos/desk/data/court_artifacts/NCLH_COURT_BLUE_202608180130.md",
     "note": null
    },
    {
     "from": "ADJUDICATE",
     "to": "KILLED",
     "utc": "2026-08-18T01:34:29Z",
     "artifact": null,
     "note": "ADJUDICATED eco-tourism thesis court: WATCH"
    }
   ],
   "context": "CONTRACTED-SUPPLY THESIS — NCLH (Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings). Tape $18.18, ~$8.35B, 33.1% off its 52-week high and only +25% off the low — the WEAKEST tape in the cruise complex. THESIS ROW, not a dislocation screen.\n\nWHY THIS NAME AND WHY NOW. The desk courted LIND (expedition) today and found capacity expanding while prices held perfectly — zero fare cuts across 72 itineraries — i.e. a growing market absorbing supply. THE OPEN QUESTION IS WHETHER THAT TRANSFERS TO MAINSTREAM CRUISE, AND THE PRIOR IS THAT IT DOES NOT, because the supply mechanics are structurally different:\n  * EXPEDITION supply is OPPORTUNISTIC — small hulls, and Lindblad literally CHARTERS a competitor's ship for three years. It arrives only when someone chooses to add it, so absorption at full fare means demand is outrunning supply.\n  * MAINSTREAM supply is CONTRACTED at shipyards years ahead — a handful of yards, booked slots, non-cancellable. That wave lands whether or not demand is there when it arrives. Pre-committed expansion is a different object from demand-responsive expansion.\nAND THE TAPE ALREADY SEPARATES THEM: LIND -6.4% off its high and VIK -9.3%, against RCL -17.7%, CCL -18.5% and NCLH -33.1%. The market is pricing something specific about mainstream. Determine WHAT, and whether it is right.\n\nTHE CENTRAL TEST IS THE SAME ONE AND IT IS EASIER HERE: DOES PRICE HOLD AS THE SUPPLY LANDS? Mainstream operators DISCLOSE net yield and net per diem quarterly — the price series is in the filings, so no scraping is required. (Note: I probed royalcaribbean/carnival/ncl/viking booking sites and NONE expose the Next.js payload Lindblad does, so the expedition_inventory connector does NOT port. If you want a forward booking read you must find another surface or say UNVERIFIED.)\n\nWHAT THE VERDICT MUST CONTAIN:\n(1) THE ORDERBOOK, FROM PRIMARY — this is the load-bearing fact and it must not be assumed. NCLH's newbuild commitments are disclosed in the 10-K/10-Q: how many hulls, which years, what berth growth as a PERCENTAGE of the existing fleet, and what committed capex and financing sit behind them. Then the same for the INDUSTRY (RCL/CCL/MSC), because it is industry berth growth that sets pricing, not NCLH's own.\n(2) NET YIELD AGAINST THAT WAVE: the disclosed net yield / net per diem trend, and guidance. Is price holding, and is any of the 'yield' actually onboard-spend mix rather than ticket price? Decompose it.\n(3) LEVERAGE — NCLH is the most levered of the three and the smallest. Quantify net debt/EBITDA, the maturity wall, covenant headroom, and what a 5% yield decline does to equity value. That asymmetry is why this name is down 33% while RCL is down 18%.\n(4) IS THE -33% ALREADY THE ANSWER? Per the value-ladder flow gate, do not bid into a re-rating that is still in progress. Establish whether the de-rate is complete, ongoing, or rational.\n(5) DIVERGENCE, NOT COVERAGE: this is heavily covered paper — that is FINE and is not a kill. Extract the STREET'S net-yield forecast for the delivery years AND its dispersion, put ours beside it, and name the mechanism for any gap. If we agree with the street, say so and kill it on that basis.\n(6) TERMS vs PRICE (the LIND mechanic): cruise lines concede via onboard credit, free-at-sea packages, reduced deposits and airfare bundling long before they cut headline fare. A stable advertised fare with escalating included-amenity value IS a price cut. Look for it.\n\nLESSONS FROM THE LIND COURT, 2026-08-18 — these are BINDING, and one is a correction of the adjudicator:\n* REFUTING A MOAT IS NOT A BEAR CASE. Capacity expansion falsifies a supply-cap premise but says nothing bad about a business — operators add capacity because they expect demand. THE TEST THAT SEPARATES THEM IS THE PRICE SERIES: absorbed expansion at intact pricing is a GROWING MARKET; expansion PLUS discounting is the bear case. Do not write up supply growth as negative without checking price first.\n* PRICE vs TERMS CONCESSIONS ARE DIFFERENT ANIMALS. An operator can concede on TERMS (deposit easing, payment plans, free cancellation) while leaving headline price untouched — invisible in any price series, but it shifts working-capital float and cancellation risk onto the operator. Lindblad had ZERO fare cuts across 72 itineraries and a universal '50% Reduced Deposit'. Classify every promotion as FARE or TERMS.\n* A MOAT THE ISSUER BOUGHT IS PRICED. If the allegedly scarce asset was acquired in a disclosed deal, that price caps it (LIND paid $17M for the Galapagos permit vehicle -> franchise ~2-7% of EV). Sibling tells: renting the 'scarce' capacity from a competitor, and allocating capital AWAY from it.\n* DIVERGENCE, NOT COVERAGE (§DIVERGENCE-NOT-COVERAGE): never kill on analyst count. Extract the STREET'S LOAD-BEARING NUMBER for the operative period AND its range, state ours beside it with a CHECKABLE MECHANISM. The kill is 'we agree with the street'. LIND died on exactly this.\n* SIZE IS NOT A DISQUALIFIER: compute days-to-build as position/(1% of ADV) against a ~$1.03M book before any unownability claim.\n* FETCH DISCIPLINE: desk/sec_fetch.py DIRECT. r.jina.ai truncates 10-Qs BEFORE the notes and produced two false absence findings in one day. UNVERIFIED beats a fetch failure dressed as a finding.\n* If a desk connector returns nothing or errors, say DEGRADED and name it — do not record an infrastructure failure as an analytic absence."
  },
