  {
   "ticker": "KNSL",
   "stage": "DONE",
   "source": "thesis:regime_drawdown_20260910",
   "enqueued_utc": "2026-09-10T14:54:41Z",
   "screen_row": {
    "ticker": "KNSL",
    "yf": "KNSL",
    "thesis": "A specialty insurer sold as a growth multiple during a front-end rate rally that mechanically RAISES the reinvestment yield on its float - its earnings power went up while its price went down.",
    "kill": "Excess-and-surplus pricing turns negative (a soft market) - if rate adequacy is deteriorating, cheap-versus-history is a value trap and the higher reinvestment yield does not compensate.",
    "expression": "Cash equity long. Note implied vol 31.4% vs realized 29.0% is the ONLY name here where implied exceeds realized, but at the 8th percentile of its 52-week range it is too low to be worth selling - no premium selling.",
    "in_book": true,
    "context": "Down 4.9% in the window, down 24.3% from a 52-week high of 484.08, at roughly 11.7x earnings (176M net income in the June quarter, 22.8M shares, live snapshot 361.82) against a business that historically traded 30-50x. No 8-K and in fact NO EDGAR filings at all since 8/25 - nothing changed. The mis-sort mechanism: the 2-year Treasury rose 19 basis points and the front end reprices what an insurer earns on float, so a rate rally is an earnings tailwind for a short-tail underwriter; instead it traded as a high-multiple growth name and was sold with that bucket. Corroborating the 'nothing is happening' read, its implied volatility sits at the 8th percentile of the last 52 weeks - the option market sees no event. Broader context from the diagnosis: the book's entire rate-sensitive cluster was FLAT TO POSITIVE over this window (WAL -0.4%, DFIN +0.5%, BBD +8.1%, WF +2.0%, HRTG +1.9%), which refutes the premise that the drawdown was a rates event hitting the financials book, and KNSL at -4.9% is the only real casualty in that cluster. Prize 6-12mo: bear 290 (0.24) / base 400 (0.51) / bull 480 (0.25)."
   },
